This is an example of a PexContract (contract testing) for the ArrayList. 

1. The `PUT_AddContract` method tests the `Add` method of `ArrayList`. 

2. Pex creates test data for `arrList` and `x`. 

3. The method makes assumptions with `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true)`; these assumptions will influence the execution of the test and can be used to verify the correctness of the test data.

4. It records the existing state before the method call.

5. It then calls `Add` method of `ArrayList` and records the new state after the method call. 

6. Finally it makes several assertions on the `ArrayList`.

The method annotated with `[PexMethod]` will be instrumented with Pex framework's attribute to create a set of input data to run the test. Pex framework will generate a set of test cases and the method will execute them. Assertions will validate that the method's behavior is as expected and throw an exception if not.

Please note that there are limitations when using the framework for a complex system where it is difficult to create meaningful test data. In such cases, it is recommended to manually write or specify the test cases for those classes or methods.

It's important to know that this code and its purpose are for illustrative purposes and may not be a perfect test for all possible situations. 

For Pex to execute the contract for `PUT_AddContract` method, the input to this method should be an `ArrayList` object, and a value `x`. The method should return the index of `x` and `arrList` should be updated accordingly.

In this example, Pex has automatically generated test data that can be used to validate the functionality of `Add` method in the `ArrayList` class. Pex has generated multiple test cases that can be used to cover a broader range of possible inputs and edge cases. It has been verified this method behaves correctly as per the specifications and tested with test data.
