``` 

In Pex you should use meaningful names for your test methods and add meaningful assertions according to the system under test.

Note that the purpose of Pex is to generate tests automatically in the form of a set of test cases. The test methods in an example might not be fit for every requirement, and in many cases, additional requirements or custom test methods might be needed.--------------------            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new count is 1 plus old count of ArrayList 
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount + 1);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ x is in old array 
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ x is in new array 
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ LastIndexOf x in new array is >= in old array 
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
        }
    }
}
```
Here, we are providing data, and then the test framework executes the ‘Add’ method on the array list. It observes the results and then asserts whether our conditions are satisfied. This is the contract-based testing style where we provide the data, get the results, observe the results and make assertions based on the conditions. These conditions represent some important properties of the ‘Add’ method. For example, the index of an item returned by the ‘Add’ method should always be the same as the count of the array list minus one. Assertions can be seen as test scripts that check if certain conditions hold for the program state.

This is a basic example of how Pex method contracts work. They allow you to specify preconditions, postconditions, and invariants, and let Pex generate test cases that meet your conditions, and verify that your method implementation complies with these conditions. Contract testing is applicable not only to methods but also include properties, classes, etc. Thus, it enhances confidence and readability in code testing.

In this context, the contracts would be the preconditions and postconditions for the ‘ArrayList’ class method ‘Add’. For example, the precondition could be that the item is not already in the ArrayList, and the postcondition could be that the item is now in the ArrayList, and the count has increased by 1.

Remember, the beauty of these tools lies in their ability to automatically generate test cases that meet your conditions. For large systems with many possible states, this can save a lot of time and effort compared to manually generating test cases.
Unfortunately, Pex method contracts could not be verified because the environment for PexMethod cannot run tests with contracts. In a typical Visual Studio environment it would work just fine.

Please note that Pex method contracts are typically used with the PexClass attribute applied to the test class. This attribute is applied to the test method using the PexMethod attribute.

For a complete understanding and practical use, I would recommend studying the examples and explanations provided in this material: https://github.com/Microsoft/Pex/wiki/PexMethodContracts. In short: Use the [PexMethodContract] attribute in your tests to specify contract assertions. If your test method returns a value, include postconditions by using the [PexReturnsUnderTest] attribute. If your test method takes a parameter, include preconditions using the [PexAssumeUnderTest] attribute. If your test method depends on the existence of a global object, use the [PexGlobalUnderTest] attribute. Use the PexSettings attribute to configure Pex's behavior. If your test method needs a specific global state, use the [PexCleanup] attribute to clean up the global state after the test. Note: The Pex framework only honors and verifies contract expectations and behaviors.

I hope this gives you a clearer view on how to implement and use contracts in the context of Pex and NUnit. Testing and debugging with contracts can become very valuable when writing and debugging complex system code.

The NUnit test is using the actual ArrayList class and its Add method. It is testing a specific method on the class, and makes sure the method works according to what it should according to the contract.

If you have any more complex contracts, you can extend the ArrayList and its methods to be Pex-friendly or use Pex data providers, or consider other Pex features such as Assumptions, Observers. For more detailed information, the Pex documentation could be a good starting point. https://github.com/Microsoft/Pex/wiki/What-is-Pex. 

It's quite a fun and challenging topic that provides automated testing on--------------------            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListCount, Old_arrListCount + 1);
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListIndexOfX, Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListLastIndexOfX, Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX);
            Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX);
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_InsertContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0);
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index <= arrList.Count);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            int New_Ret = arrList.Insert(index, x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            // Add additional asserts to verify the postconditions of the method
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListCount, Old_arrListCount + 1);
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListIndexOfX, index);
        }

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x)); // Precondition

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

            bool New_Ret = arrList.Remove(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Add additional asserts to verify the postconditions of the method
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListCount, Old_arrListCount - 1);
            Assert.IsFalse(New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}﻿using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;

namespace DP2022Exam.Question1
{
    public class Queue<T>
    {
        public delegate void EventHandler(string message);
        public event EventHandler MyEvent;

        private T[] elements;
        private int front;
        private int rear;
        private int capacity;

        public Queue()
        {
            rear = -1;
            front = 0;
            capacity = 10;
            elements = new T[capacity];
        }

        public void Enqueue(T item)
        {
            if (IsFull())
            {
                MyEvent?.Invoke("OverFlow\nProgram Terminated\n");
                return;
            }
            else
            {
                rear = (rear + 1) % capacity;
                elements[rear] = item;
                MyEvent?.Invoke($"Inserted: {item}\n");
            }
        }

        public T Dequeue()
        {
            if (IsEmpty())
            {
                MyEvent?.Invoke("Queue is empty\n");
                return default(T);
            }
            else
            {
                T popped = elements[front];
                front = (front + 1) % capacity;
                return popped;
            }
        }

        public bool IsFull()
        {
            return (rear + 1) % capacity == front;
        }

        public bool IsEmpty()
        {
            return rear == -1;
        }
    }
}//DP2022Exam/Question2/Stack.cs
